        text: '**C++ is the language underneath almost everything you touch.** Your operating system kernel, game engines, browsers, databases, and — crucially — the firmware inside microcontrollers are written in it or its ancestor C. C++ gives you two things at once that almost nothing else does: **near-zero runtime overhead** and **high-level abstractions**. You write `std::vector<int>` and get automatic resizing and bounds-checked access, yet a tight loop over it compiles to the same machine code a hand-written C loop would.\n\nFor embedded systems this matters twice. Microcontrollers have kilobytes of RAM and megabytes of flash — every byte and every instruction counts. C++ lets you write safe, readable abstractions that still compile to exactly the instructions you want. When you write a class that wraps a hardware register, the compiler can optimise the wrapper away entirely — you get the abstraction in your source and the efficiency in your binary.\n\nC++ is also **massive but layered**. You can write C-style code on day one and add classes, templates, and modern idioms as you grow. That layered learning is why the language survives: a beginner can be productive quickly while an expert can still express hardware-level control. Modern C++ (C++11 onward) added `auto`, smart pointers, lambdas, and move semantics that make the language both safer and faster than the C++98 your elders remember.',
