        text: 'Your C++ source is not run directly — it is **translated into machine code in four stages** by a compiler such as `g++` or `clang++`. Understanding these stages turns confusing compiler errors into a map of where to look.\n\n**1. Preprocessing** (`g++ -E`): the preprocessor expands every line starting with `#`. `#include <iostream>` pastes in the entire header file (that is why a one-line include can add thousands of lines). `#define` performs text substitution. The result is one giant "translation unit".\n\n**2. Compilation** (`g++ -S`): the compiler translates the C++ into assembly language for your specific CPU. This is where type errors, syntax errors, and most semantic errors are reported. The compiler also runs **optimisations** here (`-O2`).\n\n**3. Assembly** (`g++ -c`): an assembler converts assembly into **object code** (`.o` files) — raw machine instructions with placeholders for function calls that are defined elsewhere.\n\n**4. Linking** (`g++` with no flags): the linker stitches your object files together with libraries (`libstdc++`, `libc`) and resolves all the placeholders into final addresses, producing an **executable**. "Undefined reference" errors — the most confusing class — come from this stage: you *declared* a function but never provided its body.\n\nCompile with warnings on, always: `g++ -Wall -Wextra -std=c++17 prog.cpp -o prog`. Warnings are free bug reports.',
