        text: 'C++ began as "C with classes" and grew into a language with **far more expressiveness** while keeping C\'s efficiency. If you know C, you already know most C++ syntax — the differences are additions, not rewrites.\n\n**What C++ adds over C:**\n\n- **Classes & objects** — data + the functions that operate on it, bundled with access control.\n- **Templates** — write one function that works for any type instead of copy-pasting for `int`, `double`, `MyStruct`.\n- **Function overloading** — several functions with the same name, chosen by argument types.\n- **References** — safer aliases that cannot be null, unlike pointers that can dangle.\n- **Namespaces** — avoid the name collisions that plague large C codebases.\n- **Exceptions & RAII** — structured error handling and guaranteed cleanup.\n- **The STL** — ready-made containers (`vector`, `map`, `string`) and algorithms (`sort`, `find`).\n\n**What C++ keeps from C:** the syntax, the pointer model, the manual memory control, and compatibility — most valid C code is valid C++. That is why the two coexist in embedded: drivers in C, application logic in C++.\n\n**A crucial mindset shift:** in C, you think in *procedures* — "call this function, mutate this struct". In C++, you think in *objects* — "this object knows how to do its job". The `FILE*` + `fopen/fclose` dance becomes `std::ifstream` whose destructor closes the file automatically.',
        code: '// C style: free functions + explicit cleanup\nFILE* f = fopen("data.txt", "r");\n// ... read ...\nfclose(f);\n\n// C++ style: RAII does the cleanup for you\n#include <fstream>\nstd::ifstream f("data.txt");\n// ... read ...  // closed automatically when f goes out of scope',
