        text: 'A **namespace** is a named scope that groups identifiers, preventing collisions between code from different libraries. Without namespaces, two libraries both defining a `Logger` class would clash. With them, you write `network::Logger` and `console::Logger`.\n\nYou define a namespace with:\n\n```cpp\nnamespace engine {\n    int tick = 0;\n    void update() { /* ... */ }\n}\n\nengine::tick = 1;   // access with the :: scope operator\nengine::update();\n```\n\n**The `std` namespace** holds everything from the C++ standard library: `std::cout`, `std::string`, `std::vector`, `std::sort`. Because you use it constantly, the temptation is `using namespace std;` at the top of every file. **Resist it in header files** — it leaks into every file that includes the header, silently hijacking names and producing baffling ambiguities. In small `.cpp` files it is tolerated, but the professional habit is:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nusing std::cout;     // pull in just what you use\nusing std::endl;\n```\n\n**Nested namespaces** (`namespace a::b { }` in C++17) and **namespace aliases** (`namespace fs = std::filesystem;`) keep long names short. When you write a library, always wrap it in a namespace — your users will thank you.',
