        title: 'Final Project & Certification Review',
        text: 'The capstone ties every section together: a **real embedded C++ project** you design, build, and defend — exactly what the course certification checks.\n\n**A solid capstone design — the "system clock" example:**\n\n1. **Requirements** — display HH:MM:SS on an LCD, settable via buttons, time kept across reset using RTC. Constraints: no heap, 8 KB RAM, 64 KB flash.\n2. **Architecture** — a `Clock` class (RAII: constructor configures the RTC, destructor disables it), a `Button` class with debouncing (Section 6), an `Lcd` driver (volatile register or GPIO bit-banging, Sections 4, 20), and a `main` loop that polls buttons and refreshes the display.\n3. **Build** — split into modules (`clock.h/.cpp`, `button.h/.cpp`, `lcd.h/.cpp`), compile with `-Wall -Wextra -std=c++17 -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti`, and a `CMakeLists.txt`. Each module tested in isolation on the host before flashing.\n4. **Review checklist** (the certification rubric):\n   - **Encapsulation** — is every register write hidden behind a class method? (Section 11)\n   - **RAII** — do peripheral drivers configure in constructors and release in destructors? (Section 12)\n   - **const/constexpr** — are magic numbers `constexpr` and read-only accessors `const`? (Section 3)\n   - **Modern idioms** — `auto`, range-for, `std::array`, structured bindings where they fit (Section 19)?\n   - **No leaks / no UB** — no raw `new` (placement new only at fixed addresses), bounds checked (Section 9), `volatile` on every register (Section 20).\n   - **Compose, don\'t inherit** — does the `Clock` *use* an RTC rather than *inherit* one? (Section 13)\n   - **Testing** — did you verify edge cases: button bounce, mid-update register reads, compiler at `-O2`?\n\n**The discipline that gets the certificate:** review against the rubric in writing, build incrementally (each module compiles and passes a host test before the next), and test at `-O0` *and* `-O2` — differences usually expose missing `volatile`.\n\n**Final revision map:** Sections 1–3 (toolchain, types, const), 6–9 (control flow, loops, arrays), 11–12 (classes, RAII), 14 (polymorphism — know *when not* to use it), 16 (templates), 17–18 (containers, algorithms, smart pointers), 19 (move, modern features), and this week (bare-metal patterns). You now have the full toolkit to write production-quality C++ that fits in kilobytes.',
        code: '// the review rubric, as code:\nclass Clock {\n    Rtc rtc_;                  // RAII: configures in ctor\n    std::array<Button, 3> buttons_{Button(PIN_A), Button(PIN_B), Button(PIN_C)};\npublic:\n    Clock() : rtc_(/* crystal freq */) {}\n    void tick() {\n        // poll buttons (debounced), update time, drive LCD\n    }\n};\n// constexpr pin map, no magic numbers, volatile in drivers',
